From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] venc: a failed core keeps the buffers of its channels

When the reset after a stalled frame fails (patch 0029), the core may
still read or write the buffers of any instance on it. 0029 keeps the VB
pools, but destroying a channel still closed its instance and freed the
buffers the encoder allocates itself: the instance's work buffer
(VPU_EncClose), the bitstream and report buffers, ROI, CTU QP and SEI
buffers, and the source and reconstruction frames outside the common VB.
Another driver could then be given that memory while the core writes it.

cviVEncClose now leaves the instance open and every buffer allocated when
the instance's core is marked failed; the channel itself is destroyed,
and the encoder state holding the buffers stays until the system restarts.
The core takes no new instance, so nothing else frees them. A channel on
a healthy core is closed as before.

---
--- a/interdrv/cvi_vc_drv/vcodec/sample/main_enc_cvitest.c
+++ b/interdrv/cvi_vc_drv/vcodec/sample/main_enc_cvitest.c
@@ -5789,6 +5789,20 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/* A core whose reset failed may still read or write any buffer of an
+	 * instance on it: the work buffer VPU_EncClose would free, the
+	 * bitstream, report, ROI, QP and SEI buffers, the source and
+	 * reconstruction frames. The channel goes, but the instance and every
+	 * buffer stay allocated, with pTestEnc holding them, until the system
+	 * restarts (the VB pools are kept likewise). Nothing else frees them:
+	 * the core takes no new instance. */
+	if (venc_core_failed[pTestEnc->coreIdx]) {
+		CVI_VC_ERR("encoder: core %d did not reset; the channel's buffers stay until restart\n",
+			   pTestEnc->coreIdx);
+		LeaveVcodecLock(pTestEnc->coreIdx);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 #if CACHE_ENCODE_HEADER
 	if (pTestEnc->bEncHeader) {
 		for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(pTestEnc->headerBackup); i++) {
